{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:labor:reconciler","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:42cc7b64a371388c9216c89b7e09f2e6a6c1943f0b3b90b9fcd1365cd30ceb13","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":1.1},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.9},{"probability":0.9,"value":2.9}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Wave 1 produced 1.8/1.9/1.9 from three lenses, which is shared-input convergence rather than triangulation; Wave 2 split 2.1 (red) and 1.8 (Fermi). I rebuild from the component that each panelist argued best.\nWEIGHTS. The Fermi panelist's reconciliation is the only one with a line-by-line PPP ledger (China ~$41T, OECD-38 ~$78T, combined ~$119T -> China 0.345), frozen at 2025 per the accounting lock, which lowers the composite ~5-10bp against the IMF's own chain-weighted practice. I use 0.66 OECD / 0.34 China and down-weight the Reference-Class panelist's 0.30, which biased his own composite down ~7bp.\nOECD-38 LEG = 1.15%. I ADOPT the Fermi panelist's cancellation result, which is the single cleanest piece of arithmetic produced in this effort and which resolves a genuine Wave-1 standoff: the Mechanism panelist's Baumol reallocation debit on MEASURED aggregate TFP is -0.13pp/yr (0.32pp/yr of hours moving into a sector at ~0.6x average output per hour, independently re-derived) and the Auditor's healthy-aging attribution credit is +0.10pp/yr (growth accounting has no cognitive- or physical-capacity adjustment, so the \"70 in 2022 = 53 in 2000\" gain lands in the Solow residual and is published as TFP). Net -0.03pp. Each of the two saw one leg and neither saw the other. So: per-capita 0.60 (2A) + population 0.25 + AI-Q6-implied expected TFP lift +0.30 (0.162x0.70 + 0.472x0.35 + 0.365x0.05) - 0.03 net reallocation/attribution - 0.05 frictions + 0.10 retirement-age hours = ~1.15%. I REJECT the red-team's 1.30: his lift came from re-adding the attribution credit without the Baumol debit, i.e. he propagated one Wave-1 tension and not its offset.\nCHINA LEG = 3.35% REPORTED. Here the red-team landed a real hit that I accept at roughly half magnitude. CQ3 resolves on NBS-reported real GDP as reprinted by the IMF. The Auditor identified the reported-versus-deflator-consistent wedge (+1 to +1.5pp) and then used 3.0% anyway against a true-growth baseline he never wrote down — an unresolved double standard, correctly named. The institutional-floor evidence (no sub-4% print in 35 years outside 2020 and 2022; 15th/16th Five-Year-Plan targets anchoring ~4.5-5% then ~4%) is a property of the resolving series. But the red-team's 3.60 extrapolates a floor estimated when true growth was 4-5% into a window where the Fermi panelist's growth accounting gives true ~2.84%; the gap a statistical system can hold widens, it does not stay constant. I take the wedge at +0.5pp on a 2.85% true path -> 3.35%.\nCOMPOSITE P50 = 0.66(1.15) + 0.34(3.35) = 0.759 + 1.139 = 1.90 -> 1.9%.\nIDENTITY I ACCEPT (and which no effort should violate): the Fermi panelist's check 0.66(-0.4) + 0.34(-2.7) = -1.18pp against 2A's stated global -1.1pp agrees to 8bp, which proves the -1.1 is already NET of the +0.4pp healthy-aging offset. Any payload that adds +0.4 to -1.1 inflates coupled growth ~0.4pp. The Auditor flagged this first; I use the net reading.\nTAILS. P10 = 1.1%: one OECD recession year plus a Chinese property/fiscal landing gives 0.66(0.30) + 0.34(2.20) = 0.95 at the instant, and a five-YEAR average that low now also requires a break in the reported-growth regime, so the left tail is shorter than the Reference-Class and Fermi panelists allowed (1.0) — that part of the red-team's argument survives. P90 = 2.9%: OECD 1.90 with the residual carrying the capacity gain, reported China 4.60 -> 0.66(1.90) + 0.34(4.60) = 2.82, plus ~8bp of chain-weight slack. The distribution is now mildly RIGHT-skewed (upper sigma 0.78, lower 0.62) rather than left-skewed as Wave 1 had it, and the reason is purely a resolving-series property: the reported-China floor truncates the left and the reported-China wedge extends the right.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:51.782Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"OECD.Stat publishes OECD-38 PPP-GDP-weighted core inflation for calendar year 2029 with Turkiye and Mexico country components separately identified, and the aggregate prints below 2.2% while G7 core prints at or above 2.0% (i.e. the composition wedge is under 0.2pp).\",\"by_when\":\"Jun 30, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Destroys the single most load-bearing fact in my payload. My CQ2 OECD conjunct falls from 0.69 to ~0.45 and CQ2 from 42 to ~29; CQ6-C falls from 26 to ~16 with the mass going to B (~55) and D (~14); hypothesis plank (c) rises from 26 to ~35 because the back half then needs genuine mechanism and the index no longer rescues it; CQ5's inflation-salient regime weight falls from 0.38 to ~0.28, taking CQ5 from 30 to ~24.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"NBS reports a calendar-year real GDP growth rate below 3.5%, absent a declared pandemic or war shock, as reprinted in the IMF WEO.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the reported-series floor at the half-magnitude I accepted it. CQ3 P50 falls from 1.9% to ~1.75% and P90 from 2.9 to ~2.75, restoring the left-skew I removed; CQ6-A falls from 16 to ~11 and D rises from 9 to ~13; CQ4 P50 falls ~10bp on the lower-g channel; hypothesis (d) falls from 16 to ~11. This is the cleanest single falsifier of my largest update.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"ICE BofA MOVE trailing five-year average sits below 90 while the US 10-year nominal Treasury yield averaged at or above 4.25% over the same five years (i.e. the floor does NOT shift with the yield level).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the bp-normalization argument on which my half-move toward the red-team rests. CQ5's conjunct A falls from 0.48 to ~0.30 and CQ5 from 30 to ~20, i.e. back onto the Wave-1 centre; my CQ4 +80bp term-premium term weakens and P90 falls from 3.3 to ~3.0; CQ6-C loses ~3pp to B. Conversely a trailing five-year average above 115 with the same yields takes CQ5 to ~40 and vindicates the red-team.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat SES 2030 wave (published ~2032-33) plus US BLS OES show cumulative real median gross hourly earnings growth for NACE/ISIC F+Q exceeding J+K+M by more than +6.0pp over the 2022-2030 interval, with a published decomposition showing within-job wage growth rather than composition dominating.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my -6.0pp composition wedge (it would imply nearer -2pp) and with it the central reason CQ1 sits at 16. CQ1's second conjunct rises from 0.22 to ~0.42 and CQ1 from 16 to ~26, vindicating the red-team's dilution-contradiction attack at full strength; hypothesis (a) rises from 4 to ~10; CQ6 shifts ~4pp from D into B. A gap below +1.0pp, or negative, takes CQ1 to ~9 and plank (a) to 2.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD International Migration Outlook reports combined annual OECD-38 entries of foreign-trained nurses, aides and personal-care workers above 1.0M/yr in any single year, with mean time-to-licensure for an India- or Philippines-trained nurse below 12 months in at least three of the US, Germany, Japan, Canada and the UK.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the 400-700k/yr administrative throughput cap that both my dilution arithmetic and my Baumol drag depend on, and does so in a way that cuts BOTH my CQ1 legs: the care constraint then clears on migration rather than on wages or rationing, so the F+Q median is diluted harder (conjunct 2 to ~0.12, CQ1 